  "notes": "\"Hillbilly rock\": a Bakersfield-and-rockabilly band led by a mandolinist/Telecaster player, and the entry calls it the most historically literate rhythm section in the section. The era covers both stated windows — the 1990–1994 MCA hit run and the 2002-onward Fabulous Superlatives era — in one span, because the drum grammar the entry documents is the same across them. The drum block is the entry's Bakersfield reading verbatim: \"heavy use of electric instrumentation and backbeats\" and \"a big drum beat\", meaning \"a straight 8th snare stream with the backbeat hit hard and the kick two-beat, played by a drummer who also sings ... so the pattern is deliberately simple and unbroken\". That is the loudest accent tier paired with the lowest fill activity in the radio wing: `velocityTiers.accent` 110–127, `kick.densityPerBar` a flat 2 on anchors 1 and 3, `hihat.fillDensity` 0.05, and `fills` pushed out to 16/16 so the pattern really does not break. Keys are the stated A/E/G/D; harmony is I–IV–V with V7, I–I7–IV (carried in `extensions.seventh`), twelve-bar frames on the rockabilly cuts and I–♭VII–IV where the mandolin leads. The break form is the stated \"traded break (Tele, mandolin, steel in 4- or 8-bar segments)\". ⚠ INFERRED, and the entry flags the reason: \"medium-low on tempo — no per-track BPM readings were reachable for him\", so while 70–170 is the entry's own stated band, `bpm.mode` 155 is this author's placement of it inside the fast catalogue the entry names as the modelled identity, and it is marked [LOW-CONF] there. ⚠ Also INFERRED: velocity tiers, the −4 ms snare offset, `percs` contents and every melody, countermelody and bassline number. The sourced half of the entry — palette, lineage and the B-Bender mechanism behind the pedal-steel-imitating bends — is rated medium-high and shows up here only as `timbreHint`, which the engine does not read.",
